{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1791119177,"nonce":"k+iXfpJwwbXr32QfEPeucTl38pI=","target":"b0b7728d2153fa147b757de56303a97bd8f77a131721f571a89008da404a30a9","message":"A built-in \u003ccanvas\u003e element with an immediate-mode drawing context, 2D and 3D, rendered on the GPU through ashui's own pipeline. It is the foundation for charts, and for anything else that draws rather than lays out.\n\nThe model is Blinc's canvas element and DrawContext, with blinc_canvas_kit's Painter2D, Sketch, CanvasKit (2D infinite canvas) and SceneKit3D (3D scene viewer) above it. The names follow Blinc's, not HTML's canvas API. It is built Haxe-first, with shaders in hxsl.\n\n- `\u003ccanvas draw={(ctx, bounds) -\u003e ...}\u003e`. `ctx` is a DrawContext:\n  - Transform stack: pushTransform, popTransform.\n  - Clips: pushClip with a rect, rounded rect, circle or path; popClip. Opacity: pushOpacity, popOpacity.\n  - fillRect and strokeRect (with corner radius), fillCircle, strokeCircle.\n  - fillPath and strokePath, taking a Path and a Stroke (width, cap, join, miter limit, dash) and a Brush (solid, linear or radial gradient).\n  - drawText and drawImage later.\n- Path: moveTo, lineTo, quadTo, cubicTo, arcTo, arc, rect, roundedRect, circle, ellipse, close; also built from SVG path data.\n- Reactive in Solid style: `draw` runs inside a tracked computation and runs again only when a signal it read changes. Size changes also redraw it.\n- Painter, after Painter2D: the current fill and stroke, push and pop, rect, roundedRect, circle, line, path.\n- Sketch: `setup` and `draw(ctx, t, dt)` every frame, clocked by AnimationScheduler, so recordings and tests are deterministic. State lives in the sketch object. A Player extension point covers timeline content.\n- 3D in the same context, as in Blinc: setCamera, drawMesh, addLight, setEnvironment. A SceneKit3D-like layer goes above it: an orbit camera, geometry primitives and a material builder.\n\nA canvas draws at its own place in paint order with the GPU itself; the vector API and 3D are clients of that hand-off.\n1. blinc_abi emits one PRIM_CANVAS placeholder record over a canvas node's content box, as image_record does for SVGs, through a `blinc_tree_set_canvas(node, slot)` setter. The record carries the node's transform, clips, fades, clip-path, opacity and layers.\n2. When Renderer.draw reaches that record, it ends the current batch and calls the canvas's paint hook with a frame, then restores its own pipeline and bindings. The frame gives:\n   - the hlwgpu device, queue and render pass encoder, plus the target format;\n   - the node's local-to-screen transform and on-screen scale;\n   - the canvas's record index;\n   - a bind helper that attaches the frame uniforms and the records texture to a UiShader pipeline.\n3. Clipping awareness:\n   - the scissor is set to the canvas's clipped screen rect, so any draw is clipped to it;\n   - a shader that implements UiShader and uses the canvas's record index gets that record's exact clip product, through the prelude and one `canvasClip()` call: rounded clips, clip-paths, fades and opacity, as boxes and images get them.\n4. Layer mode, opt in: the canvas renders into its own texture, with a depth attachment when asked, and the placeholder composites it like an image record. This is for 3D, and for shaders that do not clip themselves.\n\nA DrawContext records vector draws and plays them through a PathShader client:\n- rects, rounded rects and circles as SDF box records;\n- paths as tessellated triangles with fringe coverage, fetched by vertex index or from a vertex buffer, and clipped with canvasClip().\n\nLayer mode with depth; a mesh pipeline with vertex and index buffers and PBR shading ported from Blinc's into hxsl; camera and lights; then a SceneKit3D-like layer above it.\n\n- Paths are flattened at a tolerance scaled by on-screen scale, so they stay crisp when zoomed.\n- Fills: a convex fast path as a fan, and ear clipping for simple concave polygons and their holes. Neither overlaps, so translucent fills do not double-blend.\n- Strokes are expanded to triangles, with miter, round and bevel joins and butt, round and square caps.\n- Antialiasing is a 1px feathered fringe with coverage per vertex, as NanoVG does, independent of MSAA.\n\n1. The hand-off: PRIM_CANVAS, the paint hook and frame, the scissor, canvasClip(), and layer mode. The \u003ccanvas\u003e element with reactive redraw. Pixel tests with a raw custom shader, clipped.\n2. Vector core: Path, flattening, fill and stroke tessellation, fringe. Unit tests.\n3. DrawContext over the hand-off, with a PathShader. Painter and Sketch. Snapshot and motion scenes.\n4. Charts on top (line, bar, area, pie), in ashui.components.\n5. Text and images in the context.\n6. 3D: depth, the mesh pipeline, camera and lights, then the SceneKit3D layer.\n7. CanvasKit: pan, zoom, spatial hit regions, selection and background patterns.","files":null}]}